Project Overview:
Create a custom desktop shell for NixOS running the Mango window manager using Quickshell (QML).
The core concept is to replace standard static desktop panels with a floating, dynamic "Pill". The Pill follows the user's mouse pointer globally. It acts as a minimized dashboard for modules (clock, workspaces, etc.). Upon interaction, the Pill expands into a larger, scrollable "Card" that reveals detailed widgets and controls.

1. Core Architecture & File Structure:
Enforce strict separation of concerns. The project should be divided into the following file structure:

main.qml: The entry point. Handles Wayland window instantiations (layer shell) and visual hierarchy.

theme.js / Theme.qml: A centralized singleton for all shared visual aspects.

config.js / Config.qml: User-facing configuration singleton with default fallbacks to prevent crashes on invalid user input.

logic/MouseTracker.qml: An abstraction layer specifically for handling global mouse coordinates (crucial since Wayland isolates this). Must support modular backends depending on user config (e.g., polling a custom daemon, Mango IPC, or ydotool).

logic/Kinematics.js: Handles the math for the delay, speed, and acceleration of the Pill/Card moving to the target position.

modules/: A directory containing independent QML files for each module (e.g., Clock.qml).

2. Standardized Script/Module Structure:
Every QML/JS file must follow this strict top-to-bottom layout:

Properties & Tunable Variables: All customizable QML properties at the very top.

Data Retrieval Functions: Functions bridging QML to Linux/Mango/System APIs (via Quickshell's Process or custom scripts).

Internal Logic & State: The core mechanics of the component.

UI Components: The visual QML elements.

3. UI/UX Mechanics (Pill & Card):
The shell operates in two primary visual states, behaving as an overlay (above other windows, except fullscreen):

The Pill (State 1):

Visual: A small, pill-shaped floating container. Displays modules in Icon mode only.

Movement: Follows the mouse based on polar coordinates (angle/distance) defined in the config.

Interactions:

Hover: Pauses following the mouse.

Left Click: Expands into the Card state.

Middle Click: Toggles mouse-following behavior on/off permanently.

Right Click: Passes the click down to the specific hovered module icon.

The Card (State 2):

Visual: An expanded, scrollable panel. Can display an unlimited number of modules.

Layout: Icon modules are arranged in rows (flow layout). Widget modules take up an entire row and expand to their required height.

Movement: By default, stops following the mouse once expanded.

Interactions:

Click Outside: Collapses back into the Pill state.

Pin Button (Top Right): Prevents collapsing when clicking outside.

Middle Click: Re-enables mouse-following behavior while in Card mode.

Note: The Pill and Card have separate, independent arrays of loaded modules (duplicate modules across both are allowed).

4. Module API Standard:
All files dropped into modules/ must conform to a standardized interface so they can be loaded dynamically by filename.

Each module must support up to 3 layouts, exposed via a state or displayMode property:

Icon: Minimalist. Text/Icon only (fits in Pill or inline Card row). Left-click triggers module-specific action.

Widget: Detailed view. Fits inside a standard Card row.

Card: Full takeover. Right-clicking a module's Icon or Widget expands it to replace the current Card content entirely with its own mega-widget.

No middle-click actions on modules (reserved for Pill/Card movement toggling).

